<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Milestone 7 (D41). Lives in Config/ rather than under WorkoutTracker/,
  because that folder is a buildable folder (T4) and everything inside it is
  swept into the target automatically — an entitlements file would be copied in
  as a resource as well as read as entitlements.

  background-delivery is the one that earns its place: the heart-rate rest timer
  (D43) has to keep watching the user's heart rate with the screen off and the
  app backgrounded, which is exactly when a rest happens. Verified 2026-08-22
  that the free personal team provisions all three.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.developer.healthkit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.developer.healthkit.access</key>
    <array/>
    <key>com.apple.developer.healthkit.background-delivery</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
docs/SPEC.md:62:- **Rest timer**: auto-starts on set completion; per-exercise durations (separate warmup vs. working; failure sets use the working duration) with global defaults (2:00 working / 1:00 warmup); local notification on finish. Per exercise the rest may instead be **heart-rate based** (D43): it ends when the heart rate drops below a threshold the user set, or when a max-wait cap expires, and the alarm says which. With no live reading it falls back to the standard duration and says so.
docs/SPEC.md:72:- No backend, accounts, or third-party runtime dependencies. **HealthKit and a watchOS companion are in scope from 2026-08-22 (D41)**, reopened deliberately for heart rate; everything else in this line still holds, and the app still talks to nothing but the device it is on.
docs/SPEC.md:86:- App-level preferences: unit preference (default from locale measurement system on first launch), drift-prompt suppression, global + per-exercise rest defaults, measured maximum heart rate? and birth date? (D45 — zones are marked estimated until a measured max exists, and hidden entirely without either), seeded-catalog version, notification-permission-requested marker, remembered catalog browsing state (grouping mode + active filters per surface — display only, D23)
docs/DECISIONS.md:21:| D13 | Rest timer: auto-start on completion, per-exercise durations (warmup vs. working split), notification | Strong-standard behavior; the split is a verified Strong nicety worth matching. |
docs/DECISIONS.md:50:| D41 | **HealthKit and a watchOS companion are in scope** (2026-08-22), reopening SPEC's "No backend, accounts, **HealthKit**, **Watch**, or third-party runtime dependencies" at the user's explicit request. The rest of that sentence stands unchanged: no backend, no accounts, no third-party runtime dependencies, still fully offline. Live heart rate reaches the phone two ways — a `HKWorkoutSession` on the phone (AirPods Pro 3 and any heart-rate GATT wearable) and a watchOS companion streaming over `WCSession`, because the Apple Watch is **not** a GATT peripheral to the phone and cannot be read live without an app on the wrist. | The user asked for heart rate as a core feature, and every route to it is HealthKit. Verified before committing to it (2026-08-22): the **free** Apple account provisions `com.apple.developer.healthkit`, `.access` and `.background-delivery`, so this costs no Developer Program membership; the iPhone workout-session API is `ios(26.0)`; and the system, not the app, generates active energy during a session. Surfacing the conflict rather than drifting from it is what the preamble to this file requires. |
docs/DECISIONS.md:51:| D42 | **Minimum iOS rises from 17.0 to 26.0**, superseding SPEC's "iOS 17+". | `HKWorkoutSession`'s iPhone initialiser, `HKLiveWorkoutBuilder` and `HKLiveWorkoutDataSource` are all `API_AVAILABLE(ios(26.0))` — read from the SDK headers, not assumed. The app has exactly one user, whose phone runs 26.6, so availability-gating every heart-rate path would add branches that no device will ever take. |
docs/DECISIONS.md:52:| D43 | **A heart-rate rest ends on the threshold OR on a cap, and the alarm says which.** Per exercise the rest timer is standard (D13/D22) or heart-rate: rest ends when HR drops below a user-set bpm, or when a max-wait cap (default 4:00) expires. With no live source it degrades to the standard timer and says it degraded. | The cap is not decoration: one dropped connection, one bad sample, or one genuinely brutal set and a threshold-only rule waits forever — for an alarm whose whole job is to be the thing you do not have to watch. "You recovered" and "time is up" are different facts about the set you just did, so a notification that conflates them is lying about your training. |
docs/DECISIONS.md:53:| D44 | **The finish summary is HealthKit's numbers beside the app's own**: time, active calories, average and max HR, time in zones — plus total volume, the exercises performed, and their best sets. Heart-rate rows are **omitted, never zeroed**, when no session ran. The summary values are persisted on `Workout` rather than re-queried. | Apple's summary cannot say what you lifted; this app's cannot say what your heart did. The user asked for both on one screen. `0 BPM` for a workout logged without a sensor is not a missing value, it is a false one — the same distinction D30 draws for draft sets. Persisting mirrors D23: what History shows must not change because a source later changed its mind. |
docs/DECISIONS.md:75:- Post-v1: iCloud sync, duration sets/cardio, RPE/supersets, catalog dedup. (~~drop sets~~ closed by D26; ~~Watch/HealthKit~~ **brought into scope by D41**, 2026-08-22)
