    46	| D38 | **The preset is chosen at log time**, with the machine's usual one preselected and switchable in one tap. A stale machine default (deleted, or belonging to another exercise) degrades to "none chosen". | Grips vary session to session on one machine, so freezing the choice at machine-creation would mean editing the machine to change handles. Preselection removes the tap in the common case without deciding anything the user did not (D33's shape, one feature over). |
    47	| D39 | **A bar is an input aid whose result is the total.** Wherever an entry is barbell work — the `barbell`/`smith` free-weight tags, or a machine whose catalog model is a rack or Smith — the user may pick a bar (a fixed list of standard bars, plus a custom weight); the weight field then takes the plates on **one** end and the app logs `bar + 2 × plates`. `SetRecord.weightValue` is the TOTAL in bar mode exactly as in total mode; the bar weight is stored alongside as provenance so history can show the breakdown and the next session can prefill plates rather than a total. The bar is **not** part of the D23 snapshot and not part of `RecordGroupKey`: changing bars does not split records the way changing equipment (D19) or variation (D36) does. | Two 135 lb sets are the same lift whether the number was typed or computed, so they must land in the same record table — the bar changes the *input*, not the load. And a stored weight that sometimes excluded the bar would silently drop every barbell PR by the weight of a bar with no error anywhere: `RecordsMath`, volume (D21), e1RM (D20) and the export's `weight` all read `weightValue` and know nothing about bars. The user asked for this on 2026-08-22; it is the first half of milestone 6's plate calculator (D16). |
    48	| D40 | **A bar carries its own unit, and picking one sets the row's unit.** Bars are listed in kg and lb as *distinct* bars (a 20 kg bar is not a 45 lb bar), picking one never converts anything, and the kg/lb toggle is disabled while a bar is chosen. A row already carrying a total in the bar's own unit keeps it (the total is re-read as bar + plates); a total in the *other* unit is dropped rather than reinterpreted. | D9/D25 forbid silent conversion, and the toggle's own semantics — reinterpret the number in the other unit — would turn a 20 kg bar into a 20 kg-labelled 20 lb one on a stray tap. Switching unit means picking up a different bar, which is what happens in a gym. |
    49	
    50	| D41 | **HealthKit and a watchOS companion are in scope** (2026-08-22), reopening SPEC's "No backend, accounts, **HealthKit**, **Watch**, or third-party runtime dependencies" at the user's explicit request. The rest of that sentence stands unchanged: no backend, no accounts, no third-party runtime dependencies, still fully offline. Live heart rate reaches the phone two ways — a `HKWorkoutSession` on the phone (AirPods Pro 3 and any heart-rate GATT wearable) and a watchOS companion streaming over `WCSession`, because the Apple Watch is **not** a GATT peripheral to the phone and cannot be read live without an app on the wrist. | The user asked for heart rate as a core feature, and every route to it is HealthKit. Verified before committing to it (2026-08-22): the **free** Apple account provisions `com.apple.developer.healthkit`, `.access` and `.background-delivery`, so this costs no Developer Program membership; the iPhone workout-session API is `ios(26.0)`; and the system, not the app, generates active energy during a session. Surfacing the conflict rather than drifting from it is what the preamble to this file requires. |
    51	| D42 | **Minimum iOS rises from 17.0 to 26.0**, superseding SPEC's "iOS 17+". | `HKWorkoutSession`'s iPhone initialiser, `HKLiveWorkoutBuilder` and `HKLiveWorkoutDataSource` are all `API_AVAILABLE(ios(26.0))` — read from the SDK headers, not assumed. The app has exactly one user, whose phone runs 26.6, so availability-gating every heart-rate path would add branches that no device will ever take. |
    52	| D43 | **A heart-rate rest ends on the threshold OR on a cap, and the alarm says which.** Per exercise the rest timer is standard (D13/D22) or heart-rate: rest ends when HR drops below a user-set bpm, or when a max-wait cap (default 4:00) expires. With no live source it degrades to the standard timer and says it degraded. | The cap is not decoration: one dropped connection, one bad sample, or one genuinely brutal set and a threshold-only rule waits forever — for an alarm whose whole job is to be the thing you do not have to watch. "You recovered" and "time is up" are different facts about the set you just did, so a notification that conflates them is lying about your training. |
    53	| D44 | **The finish summary is HealthKit's numbers beside the app's own**: time, active calories, average and max HR, time in zones — plus total volume, the exercises performed, and their best sets. Heart-rate rows are **omitted, never zeroed**, when no session ran. The summary values are persisted on `Workout` rather than re-queried. | Apple's summary cannot say what you lifted; this app's cannot say what your heart did. The user asked for both on one screen. `0 BPM` for a workout logged without a sensor is not a missing value, it is a false one — the same distinction D30 draws for draft sets. Persisting mirrors D23: what History shows must not change because a source later changed its mind. |
    54	| D45 | **An estimated maximum heart rate is marked as estimated wherever it reaches a screen**, until the user supplies a measured one. Zones are **55/65/75/85/95%** of max (revised 2026-08-24 — see below); with neither a measured max nor a birth date, zones are not shown at all. | 220−age carries a standard deviation of ±10–12 bpm, so an unmarked "Zone 4" asserts a precision the number does not have — exactly what D9/D25's `≈` exists to prevent one domain over. Showing no zone is honest; showing a confident wrong one is not. **Corollary, learned 2026-08-24:** a decision to hide a feature until the user supplies a basis is only honest if supplying that basis is REACHABLE. As shipped, the only way in was a button that rendered only once zones already worked, so no user could ever switch them on. Any new "hidden until configured" gate must ship its own way in — Settings, not just a contextual hint. **Boundaries revised 2026-08-24, deliberately reopening the 50/60/70/80/90 half of this decision:** after real gym use the user reported every zone reading about one too high, and chose to shift each boundary up 5 percentage points. Root cause is probably not the boundaries but the basis — they are on the 220−age estimate, which runs low for fit people, and a low max inflates every percentage. Raising the max was offered and declined in favour of the shift. **The cost, which is real: this app's "Zone 3" is no longer Polar's or Apple's Zone 3.** Zone names here are not portable, and a measured maximum would let these go back to standard. |
    55	

